# CDP Logging (ECS)

All generated logging must follow CDP's streamlined Elastic Common Schema.
Non-standard fields may end up in `broken_logs*` and will not search correctly
in OpenSearch. Docs: CDP Portal → Documentation → Logging.

Enforced by ESLint `local/cdp-logging` (`eslint-rules/cdp-logging.js`).

## Logger

- Prefer `request.logger` in handlers; otherwise `createLogger()` from
  `#src/server/common/helpers/logging/logger.js`.
- Use structured Pino: `logger.info({ ...fields }, 'message')`.
- Pass errors as `err` for ECS mapping:
  `logger.error({ err: error }, 'Failed to …')`.
- Do not use `console.log` / `console.error` for application logs.

## Allowed fields only

- Set only ✅ / ✅⚠️ CDP fields. Never set ❌ reserved fields
  (`@timestamp`, `service.name`, `service.version`, `trace.id`,
  `container_*`, `ecs_*`, response body/bytes/mime_type/response_time, etc.).
- Nest slash-path fields as objects:
  `error/message` → `{ error: { message } }`,
  `event/action` → `{ event: { action } }`.
  Flattened keys ≠ nested maps in OpenSearch.
- Literal flat dotted keys only: `host.hostname`, `log.level`, `span.id`,
  `transaction.id`.
- ✅⚠️ (`client.*`, `http.request.method`, `url.path`, …) are overridden when
  `req`/`res` are present — do not set them to fight the pipeline.

## What to put where

- Domain markers: `event.action`, `event.reason`, `event.reference`, or the
  `message` string — not invent custom top-level keys for searchable data.
- Do not set `event.category` unless it is an ECS allowlist value
  (`api`, `authentication`, `web`, …). Prefer `event.action`.
- Application logs only — not SC-controlled audit logging.
- Redact secrets / PII.

## Examples

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD
request.logger.error(
  {
    err: error,
    event: { action: 'exemption_delete_failed', reason: 'api_error' }
  },
  'Error deleting exemption'
)

request.logger.info(
  { event: { action: 'exemption_deleted', reference: exemptionId } },
  `Deleted exemption ${exemptionId}`
)

// ❌ BAD
request.logger.info({ exemptionId, foo: 'bar' }, 'deleted')
console.log('deleted', exemptionId)
logger.error(error) // use { err: error }
```
